From: Devin AI <devin@cognition.ai>
Date: Thu, 9 Jan 2026 10:37:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] SONiC-ONLY: Y2K38: Add compile-time check for 64-bit time_t

This patch adds a compile-time assertion to verify that time_t is at least
64-bit, preventing timestamp overflow on January 19, 2038 (Y2K38 problem).

FRR uses time_t extensively for timestamps in routing protocols (BGP, ISIS,
OSPF, etc.). On 32-bit systems, time_t may be 32-bit which will overflow
on January 19, 2038 at 03:14:07 UTC.

This compile-time check ensures that FRR is built on a system with 64-bit
time_t support, or fails the build with a clear error message.

Signed-off-by: Devin AI <devin@cognition.ai>
---
 lib/libfrr.h | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lib/libfrr.h b/lib/libfrr.h
index 1234567..abcdefg 100644
--- a/lib/libfrr.h
+++ b/lib/libfrr.h
@@ -10,6 +10,18 @@
 #include "sigevent.h"
 #include "vector.h"
 
+/*
+ * Y2K38 Compile-Time Check
+ *
+ * Ensure that time_t is at least 64-bit to prevent timestamp overflow
+ * on January 19, 2038 (Unix timestamp 2147483647).
+ *
+ * If this assertion fails, the system needs to be compiled with
+ * -D_TIME_BITS=64 -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64 flags or use a 64-bit platform.
+ */
+_Static_assert(sizeof(time_t) >= 8,
+    "Y2K38: time_t must be at least 64-bit to prevent timestamp overflow");
+
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 extern "C" {
 #endif
